From 0efae945f369025c00a90e7f097f747ce1741b37 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 27 Jul 2026 20:26:52 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 12/61] util/u_cpu_detect: count the CPUs on POSIX-lite
 platforms too
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The whole processor-counting block sits under DETECT_OS_POSIX, but
nothing inside it needs the whole of POSIX: each path is already guarded
by the macro that names what it uses — HAS_SCHED_GETAFFINITY,
_SC_NPROCESSORS_ONLN, _SC_NPROCESSORS_CONF, HW_NCPUONLINE. A POSIX-lite
C library that has sysconf() and defines _SC_NPROCESSORS_ONLN therefore
gets the right answer from the code that is already written; it is only
the outer guard that keeps it out.

Left out, available_cpus stays 0, nr_cpus falls to MAX2(1, 0) and every
consumer believes the machine has one core. util/u_queue.c sizes its
thread pool from that number, so the effect is a single-threaded
compiler on a multi-core device.

DETECT_OS_POSIX_LITE defaults to DETECT_OS_POSIX in detect_os.h, so
every platform that reached this block still reaches it, with the same
paths enabled. The <unistd.h> include above moves for the same reason
and in the same way.

mesa-nvk-horizon: Phase 3 item 1 (OS detection) — the CPU-count half of
  it, and the quality defect recorded in STATUS.md at the end of item 6
Why: Horizon is POSIX-lite (patch 0007: no <syslog.h>, no <sys/shm.h>,
  no dynamic loader), so it matched neither arm of the DETECT_OS_WINDOWS
  / DETECT_OS_POSIX pair and set no CPU count at all. newlib does define
  _SC_NPROCESSORS_CONF (9) and _SC_NPROCESSORS_ONLN (10) in
  sys/unistd.h:370-371, and compat/sysconf.c answers both from
  svcGetInfo(InfoType_CoreMask), so the existing sysconf path is exactly
  the one that works here.
Evidence: before this patch, the object built for aarch64/horizon
  contains no reference to sysconf at all —
    $ aarch64-none-elf-nm build/mesa-probe/.../u_cpu_detect.c.o | grep sysconf
    (no output)
  — while configure reports "Checking for function \"sysconf\" : YES",
  and HAS_SCHED_GETAFFINITY is absent ("Checking for function
  \"sched_getaffinity\" : NO"), which is why the _SC_NPROCESSORS_ONLN
  path is the one that has to be reachable. After it, the same object
  references sysconf and tests/t_threads.c checks the value it produces
  against svcGetInfo(InfoType_CoreMask) on the console.
Upstream: yes — POSIX-lite is an existing macro of Mesa's own, it
  defaults to DETECT_OS_POSIX so no current platform changes, and the
  inner guards were already written to answer for themselves.
---
 src/util/u_cpu_detect.c | 16 +++++++++++++---
 1 file changed, 13 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/util/u_cpu_detect.c b/src/util/u_cpu_detect.c
index 451881d..1f6bf34 100644
--- a/src/util/u_cpu_detect.c
+++ b/src/util/u_cpu_detect.c
@@ -73,7 +73,10 @@
 #include <elf.h>
 #endif
 
-#if DETECT_OS_POSIX
+/* For sysconf(), which is where the CPU count comes from below.
+ * POSIX-lite rather than POSIX: <unistd.h> and sysconf are part of the
+ * subset every POSIX-lite C library has. */
+#if DETECT_OS_POSIX_LITE
 #include <unistd.h>
 #endif
 
@@ -779,7 +782,14 @@ _util_cpu_detect_once(void)
       GetSystemInfo(&system_info);
       available_cpus = MAX2(1, system_info.dwNumberOfProcessors);
    }
-#elif DETECT_OS_POSIX
+/* Counting processors needs sysconf() or sysctl(), not the whole of
+ * POSIX. Every path inside is already guarded by the macro that names
+ * what it uses, so a POSIX-lite platform takes exactly the ones its C
+ * library supports and no more. Without this, such a platform sets
+ * nothing at all and nr_cpus falls to the MAX2(1, 0) below — one CPU on
+ * a machine that has several, which u_queue then sizes its thread pool
+ * from. */
+#elif DETECT_OS_POSIX_LITE
 #  if defined(HAS_SCHED_GETAFFINITY)
    {
       /* sched_setaffinity() can be used to further restrict the number of
@@ -847,7 +857,7 @@ _util_cpu_detect_once(void)
       total_cpus = ncpu;
    }
 #  endif /* DETECT_OS_BSD */
-#endif /* DETECT_OS_POSIX */
+#endif /* DETECT_OS_POSIX_LITE */
 
    util_cpu_caps.nr_cpus = MAX2(1, available_cpus);
    total_cpus = MAX2(total_cpus, util_cpu_caps.nr_cpus);
-- 
